<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicente Cerna y Cerna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicente Cerna y Cerna (1815–1885) was a Guatemalan general and conservative politician who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> as president in 1865. He continued Carrera's alliance with the church and the conservative elite but faced growing liberal opposition, and he was overthrown in 1871 by the Liberal Revolution led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicente-cerna-y-cerna.jpg" alt="Vicente Cerna y Cerna" width="240" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presidencia de Guatemala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VicenteCerna_(oficial).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vicente Cerna y Cerna was born on 22 January 1815 in Ipala, Chiquimula, where he later served as corregidor. A friend and comrade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>, he rose as an officer of the caudillo's army. As a colonel he commanded one of the Guatemalan battalions at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-arada">Battle of La Arada</a>, fought near Chiquimula on 2 February 1851 against an invading army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, which Carrera's forces defeated with 125 casualties against more than 1,500 on the other side. In 1854 he was among those who signed the act declaring Carrera president for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1863, as a brigadier general, Cerna led an army of Hondurans, Salvadorians and Guatemalans formed by Carrera into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. His forces took Cucuyagua on 10 June 1863 and Los Llanos de Santa Rosa on 15 June, occupied Gracias a Dios and declared José María Medina president of Honduras, then marched on the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a>, against the interim president José Francisco Montes Fonseca, whose forces set the town on fire before fleeing. After Carrera's victory over the Salvadorian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a> that year, Cerna was made a field marshal, a rank also given to Serapio Cruz and José Víctor Zavala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Cerna became president on 24 May 1865, after Carrera's death, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s leaders would have preferred Zavala. Church and state remained closely bound under the Concordat of 1852, and liberals were prosecuted and sent into exile, among them those who later began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution</a>. His victory over Zavala in the election of 1869 brought charges of fraud, and from then on his government faced constant uprisings.</p><p>Liberal writers such as Alfonso Enrique Barrientos later described it as an archaic regime that concentrated all power in him. With reinforcements sent by the Mexican president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a>, the liberal forces defeated his troops at Totonicapán on 23 June and at San Lucas Sacatepequez in June 1871. He died on 27 June 1885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vicente Cerna y Cerna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Liberal Revolution of 1871, led by Miguel García Granados and Justo Rufino Barrios, overthrew Carrera’s successor <b>Vicente Cerna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jutiapa, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.28,-89.89&amp;year=1860" aria-label="Jutiapa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vicente Cerna y Cerna"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5009 3791h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5009 3791h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.28,-89.89&amp;year=1860">What was here in 1860? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
